## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are attracting tremendous research interest because of their rich redox sites and high specific area which are beneficial for the energy storage applications. Nevertheless, the poor conductivity, low mechanical strength and unsatisfactory capacity severely hinder their wide application. Hence, it is of practical significance to design highly efficient and facile strategy to solve these issues. Herein, vertically oriented ZnO nanorod arrays are applied as precursor to synthesize laminated scale-like and highly-oriented Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO nanocomposite. Owing to the desirable conductivity resulting from the doping nickel ions and the interaction between ZnO and its relative MOF, the fabricated 0.3Ni/Zn
MOF/ZnO@CC electrode exhibits an electrochemical capacitance of $1693\ \mathrm{\mF\cm^{-2}}$ at $1\mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Moreover, the electrochemical capacitance retention of $80.7\%$ after 2500 cycling numbers is obtained under the constant current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ and the low internal resistance $\mathrm{R_{s}}$ of $0.89\Omega$ is observed. For practical application, the as-synthesized laminated scale-like Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC nanocomposite is served as positive electrode to fabricate solid-state asymmetric supercapacitor device. Moreover, a 2.5 V indicator could be powered for 8 minutes when the prepared supercapacitor units are connected. This work demonstrates the promising potential of the synthesized scale-like Ni/Zn-MOF composites for electrochemical energy storage applications.
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Along with the sustaining fast exploitation of conventional sources and the increasing exhaustion of nonrenewable fuel, the developments of new alternative energy sources and conversion systems have become key issues and aroused enormous research attention.1, 2 Owing to the superior energy and power densities and rate property as well as the durability compared to the traditional rechargeable batteries or other energy storage equipments, electrochemical supercapacitors are widely considered as the potential alternatives for the further energy storage devices.3-6 Notably, the properties of capacitors are usually largely depended on the intrinsic characteristics of electrode materials. Hence, developing novel electrode materials with enormous active sites and large surface areas are important issues.7-9 Metal oxides with a variety of valences and diverse structures exhibit great promising potential for achieving excellent electrochemical performances.10-14
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with porous structure and diverse morphologies have been widely employed as precursors and electrode materials for electrochemical storage devices.15-20 Typically, metal ions or clusters are connected to assigned ligands to form the three-dimensional frameworks.21-25 Plenty of nanopores can provide large surface areas and improve the electrolyte diffusion rates, leading to an enhanced electrochemical performances.26-29 Owing to their unique intrinsic properties, MOF-based electrode composites have been widely investigated and used in supercapacitor.30-33 However, conventional MOFs are usually composed with single metal element, which may not be able to meet the demand for high performance capacitors. To overcome this drawback, one efficient way is to synthesize multi-component composite through element doping.22, 34-36 Among various metal composites, zinc complex has been widely used as the electrode materials due to its security, abundance of reserves and well durability. Ju et al. prepared $\mathrm{\DeltaNi/ZnO}$ nano-sponges through optimized annealing of Ni/Zn-MOF.37 Annealing leads to the incorporation of intrinsic $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ into the surface of NiO nanoparticles, providing more reactive sites. The oxygen vacancies in $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and the heterostructure interface between NiO and $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ facilitate the charge conversion. The prepared electrode shows the specific capacitance of $807\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, Saha et al. fabricated the rGO/Zn-MOF $@$ PANI electrode and its specific capacitance could reach to $372\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .38 Additionally, Shi et al. reported the unique Co/Zn-MOF derived $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}.$ - S polyhedron homogeneously embedded between the rGO nanosheets as a binder-free electrode for high performance hybrid supercapacitor. The prepared Co/Zn-S@rGO-7 electrode shows a high capacitance of $1640\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .39
Herein, the scale-like and highly-oriented Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC composite is synthesized. The vertically-oriented $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorod arrays grown on carbon cloth were synthesized through a facile water bath method. In the presence of terephthalic acid and N, $N.$ -dimethylformamide, the synthesized ZnO nanorod arrays were recrystallized into Zn-MOF. The addition of nickel ions plays an important role on the structure formation, which can be implanted into the framework during the recrystallization procedure. Additionally, the fabricated Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode shows satisfied electrochemical performances which may be due to the unique laminated scale-like structure and the rational combination material. Typically, the 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode shows a supercapacitance of $1693~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ at the current density of $1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Moreover, the electrochemical stability retention of $80.7\%$ after 2500 cycling numbers is obtained under the constant current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and the low internal resistance ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{s}}$ of $0.89\ \Omega$ is observed. For practical application, an asymmetric supercapacitor equipment based on the as-synthesized laminated scale-like Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC composite is successfully fabricated and exhibits the power density of 170 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ at the corresponded energy density of 16.4 W h $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, when the fabricated solid-state supercapacitor units are connected in series, a 2.5V indicator could be powered for 8 minutes. Overall, this work demonstrates a promising potential of the synthesized Ni/Zn-MOF based composites for electrochemical energy storage applications.
## 2 EXPERIMENTAL SECTIONS
## 2.1 Materials
Carbon cloth was purchased from Zhongwei New Material Co., Ltd located in Shanghai. Hydrochloric acid was purchased from Fuyu Fine Chemical Co., Ltd located in Tianjin. Acetone, zinc acetate, ammonium hydroxide, ethanol, potassium hydroxide (KOH, AR), polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF, AR) zinc nitrate hexahydrate, nickel (II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , AR), terephthalic acid (PTA, AR), hexamethylenetetramine (HMTA, AR), acetylene black (AR) and activated carbon were all purchased from the Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. N, $N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, AR) was purchased from Biaoshiqi Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd located in Tianjin. All of the above chemicals were directly utilized as received without further purification.
## 2.2 Synthesis of ZnO nanorod arrays on carbon cloth (ZnO@CC)
Typically, the carbon cloth $(1.0\times1.0~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ was firstly cleaned using acetone and ethanol repeatedly to remove the surface impurity. After that, the cleaned carbon cloth was dried in vacuum oven under $50~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ adequately. A seed assisted hypothermal method was used for the synthesis of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorod-arrays on carbon cloth. Typically, the pre-treated carbon cloth was immersed into $0.005\mathrm{M}$ zinc acetate solution and then placed in 200 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $10~\mathrm{\min}$ at to obtain ZnO seed layer. Subsequently, $0.05~\mathrm{~M~}$ hexamethylenetetramine, $0.05~\mathrm{M}$ zinc nitrate hexahydrate and $2~\mathrm{mL}$ ammonium hydroxide were mixed under magnetic stirring to afford a uniform dispersed precursor solution. The resulting carbon cloth was subsequently placed into the pre-prepared solution and placed in water-bath for $4\mathrm{h}$ under $95~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ . After the reaction, the obtained white carbon cloth was cleaned with deionized water repeatedly. Later, the resulting carbon cloth was dried in vacuum oven at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to achieve the expected $Z_{\mathrm{nO}\mathcal{O}}\mathrm{CC}$ .
## 2.3 Preparation of Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC
The Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC was synthesized through a solvothermal strategy on basis of the pre-synthesized ZnO nanorod precursor. In brief, 0.067 mmol terephthalic acid was dissolved into DMF and stirred constantly to form a homogenous solution at ambient environment. Subsequently, the prepared precursor of $Z_{\mathrm{nO}\mathcal{O}}\mathrm{CC}$ was put into the above solution. After that, the pre-prepared solution was poured into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave. After reacted at $120~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , the final products were cleaned with DMF and deionized water repeatedly. The Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC was then obtained after drying.
## 2.4 Preparation of Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC
The synthesis procedure of 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC was similar to Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC, except that 0.3 mmol $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is added into the above mixed homogenous DMF solution. Additionally, 0.1 and 0.2 mmol $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were also supplied into the above mixed solution to achieve two samples for comparison, respectively. Moreover, the content of PTA was supplied based on the relationship of PTA $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}^{2+}{=}2/3$ . The obtained final solution was also poured into the Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and reacted at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\ \mathrm{~h~}$ . Then the expected 0.1Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and 0.2Ni/ZnMOF/ZnO@CC samples were obtained after drying.
## 2.5 Characterizations
The morphology of all synthesized products was analyzed using the ZEISS Supra 55 scanning electron microscope (SEM). The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM), the energy dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) elemental mapping images and the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) and were all performed based on a JEM-3010 transmission electron microscopy. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were performed using a Kratos Axis Ultra DLD spectrometer employing a monochromated Al Ka X-ray source $\mathrm{(h=1486.7~eV}$ ). The crystallographic structures of the synthesized materials were determined by X-ray diffractometer with $\mathrm{{Cu-Ka}}$ radiation. The crystal structures of the samples were measured by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) with $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K a}$ radiation $(\lambda{=}1.54056\AA)$ . The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) was performed on a FTIR spectrometer (Shi-madzu) in the form of KBr pellets.
## 2.6 Electrochemical performance tests
The electrochemical properties of the synthetic materials were tested using an electrochemical workstation. In the electrochemical three-electrode test, the synthetic material without any binder was used as the positive electrode, platinum foil as the counter electrode, and mercuric oxide as the reference electrode. 6 M KOH aqueous solution was used as the electrolyte. Typically, cyclic voltammetric curves were measured from 0 to $0.7\mathrm{V}$ at different scan rates from 10 to $100\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ , and galvanostatic charge-discharge curves were measured from 0 to $0.54\mathrm{V}$ at different current densities from 2 to $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Impedance spectral curves were measured in the frequency response range of $0.01\mathrm{Hz}$ to $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ . In addition, the electrochemical capacitance of the electrode under test can be calculated using the following equation.
$$
C_{A}=\frac{I\displaystyle\int V\Delta t}{s V^{2}}
$$
In this, $C_{A}(\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ is the areal capacitance, $I_{A}$ is the discharge current, $t$ (s) represents the discharge time, $V(\mathrm{V})$ refers to the voltage window and $s(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ is the active area of the materials.
## 2.7 Preparation of all-solid-state asymmetric supercapacitor equipment
The negative material was prepared using activated carbon, acetylene black and polyvinylidene fluoride with a mass ratio of 8:1:1. The above mixture was then dried using a vacuum oven and embedded into 1.0 $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ nickel foam. An asymmetric supercapacitor was made by employing the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and activated carbon under the assistance of KOH/PVA gelatin electrolyte. Meanwhile, little pressure is applied to support the gelatin electrolyte to make sure that it is able to form a separating layer between the electrodes. Additionally, the charges in each electrode should be equal, which can be evaluated according to the following formula.
$$
q=C\times m\times\Delta V
$$
In this, the $C(\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ refers to the capacitance, m (g) is the mass of the activated material and $V(\mathrm{V})$ refers to the potential range. According to this formula, the mass ratio of the active substance on the positive electrode to the active substance on the negative electrode can be calculated based on the following relationship:
$$
m^{+}/m^{-}=C^{-}\Delta V^{-}/C^{+}\Delta V^{+}
$$
In this, $C^{+}~(\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $C^{-}\ (\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ represents the capacitance of the positive and negative electrode, respectively. $V^{+}\left(\mathrm{V}\right)$ and $V^{\ast}(\mathrm{V})$ represents the potential of the positive and negative electrode, respectively. The capacitance $(C s)$ of the asymmetric supercapacitor can be calculated based on the equation below:
$$
C_{S}=\frac{I\displaystyle\int V\Delta t}{m V^{2}}
$$
In this, the $I$ (A) represents the current, $m\left(\mathrm{g}\right)$ is the total mass of activated materials, $t$ (s) represents the discharge time and $V(\mathrm{V})$ refers to the voltage range. The energy density of $E\left(\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\right)$ and power density of $P\left(\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg^{-1}}\right),$ ) can be calculated through the following relationship:
$$
E=\frac{1}{2}C_{s}\Delta V^{2}
$$
$$
\scriptstyle P=E/\Delta t
$$
3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS
Fig. 1. (Ⅰ) Schematic demonstration for the step-wise synthesis of laminated scale-like and highlyoriented Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC nanocomposite. (Ⅱ) SEM images of (a-c) ZnO@CC nanorod arrays, (d-f) 0.1Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC, (g-i) 0.2Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and (j-l) 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC scale-like laminated-nanosheets with different magnifications.
The step-wise synthesis of laminated scale-like and highly-oriented Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC composite via water-bath method and hydrothermal strategy is schematically illustrated in Fig. 1(Ⅰ). Typically, the ZnO nanorods are firstly vertical grown on carbon fibers via a hypothermal method and served as precursor for further use. Subsequently, Zn-MOF/ZnO nanosheets were synthesized by placing the pre-prepared ZnO nanorods into a mixture solution and reacted at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . In this process, the dissolution and reprecipitation mechanism could be used to explain this reaction. Meanwhile, laminated scale-like and highly-oriented Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO nanosheets was also synthesized with the assistant of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under the same reaction condition. The as-synthesized homogeneous laminated scale-like and highlyoriented nanosheets could provide a large surface area and enormous active centers, resulting in the enhanced reaction kinetics during the electrochemical processes.
The morphological and structural evolution of the step-wise synthesized materials were first explored using scanning electron microscope (SEM). ZnO nanorod arrays were grown on carbon cloth substrate through a facile seed-assisted solvothermal strategy. The surface of the utilized carbon cloth is very smooth which can be used as an applicable substrate for decoration. It can be observed from Fig. 1a-c, the regularly straight ZnO nanorod arrays are homogeneous grown on the entire surface of carbon fiber. A careful examination shows that the synthesized needlelike $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorod arrays are about $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in length and approximate $130\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter. Nevertheless, after the addition of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and PTA, the assynthesized Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC exhibits a homogeneous laminated scale-like and highly-oriented pony-size nanosheets morphology, which could be interpreted by the “dissolution/re-precipitation” mechanism. Meanwhile, the synthesized pony-size nanosheets tend to form a brush-like structure and inter-cross one by one to form a porous 3D framework as shown in Fig. 1g-i. In addition, different concentrations of PTA and Ni ions will lead to different densities and thicknesses of the nanosheets. The addition amount of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shows important effect in the generation of the final laminated scalelike and highly-oriented nanosheets, which is conducive to the diffusion of ions. Especially, the density and thickness of the synthesized Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC nanosheets can be regulated conveniently by the addition of the content of PTA and Ni ions to obtain an optimal structure. The synthesized laminated scales and highly oriented nanosheets form a porous three-dimensional framework, which is conducive to increase the number of active centers and further promotes the charge transfer rate for rapid redox reactions.
To further understand the chemical composition and detailed structures of the synthesized ZnO@CC nanorod arrays and Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC laminated scale-like and highly-oriented nanosheets, different analyses were performed. As shown in Fig. 2a and b, the ZnO nanorods with a smooth surface exhibits a diameter of approximately $120~\mathrm{nm}$ . The observed well-ordered lattice plane could be attributed to the (101) lattice spacing of ZnO. HRTEM images of the interlaced Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC nanosheets shown in Fig. 2d and e revealed its highly ordered lattice planes. The measured interplanar crystal distances of 0.523 nm and $0.472~\mathrm{nm}$ should belong to the (210) and (211) planes of Zn-MOF, respectively. Moreover, the synthesized Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC nanosheets also exhibits a well lattice-plane distance of $0.371\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which should be related to the (023) lattice facet of Zn-MOF, as shown in Fig. $2\mathrm{g}$ and h. Additionally, the SAED patterns shown in Fig. 2c, f and i all exhibited the well-defined diffraction spots and rings. Furthermore, the EDS and EDX spectra shown in Fig. 2j and k clearly displayed the uniform distribution of C, N, O, Ni and Zn elements on Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC nanosheets. Similarly, the corresponding spectrum also evidenced the co-existence of the detected elements. As observed from the table shown in Fig. 2l, it can be found that the atomic content of the doped nickel is low.
XRD analysis was then performed to characterize the transformation from ZnO nanorod arrays to layered Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO nanosheets. As observed in Fig. 3a, the intrinsic characteristic diffraction peaks appearing at 31.8o, 34.4o, $36.3^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $47.5^{\mathrm{o}}$ should be attributed to the (100), (002), (101) and (102) lattice planes of the synthesized ZnO (JCPDS No-361451) nanorods, respectively. In addition, the standard pattern of pure Zn-MOF (CCDC No-193934) is also shown in Fig. 3a. It was observed that both the synthesized Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC showed similar characteristic diffraction peaks compared to the standard pattern. In addition, FT-IR analysis was performed to investigate the group composition of the synthesized MOFs, as shown in Fig. 3b. It can be seen that the stretching vibration of the OH group is located at $3619\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , and the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of the - COO group are located at $1598~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and $1381~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , respectively. Moreover, stretching bands of paraaromatic CH are appear at 1502, 1153, 1105, 1022 and $825~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , respectively.2, 24, 25, 40
In addition, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were then performed to investigate the surface elemental configuration and state of the synthesized Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC in depth. The full survey spectrum of the prepared Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO (Fig. S7) demonstrated the presence of C, N, O, Ni and Zn elements. In addition, the XPS spectra after electrochemical tests are included in Fig. S1. The detailed resolved spectra of C 1s, O 1s, N 1s, Ni 2p and $Z_{\mathfrak{n}2\mathfrak{p}}$ are shown in Fig. 3c-g. Specifically, the $Z\mathfrak{n}\ 2\mathfrak{p}$ spectrum (Fig. 3f) is deconvoluted into two peaks appearing at $1045.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $1022.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which can be considered as the characteristic peaks of Zn $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ and Zn $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ .40 In the O 1s spectrum, two peaks appeared at $531.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $533.5\mathrm{eV}$ should be the contribution of the oxygen functional group, as shown in Fig. 3c. Fig. 3e demonstrates that the peak of Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ appears at $856.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ is located at $873.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ . In addition, the peaks appeared at the binding energies of $861.2\mathrm{eV}$ and $879.9\mathrm{eV}$ should represent the satellite peaks.41 The characteristic peak of N 1s (Fig. $3\mathrm{g}$ ) centered at $400.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ is supposed to be contributed by the C-N bond. Furthermore, the spectrum of C 1s in Fig. 3d can be deconvoluted into three peaks corresponding to the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-C/C=C}$ $(284.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ , C-O $(284.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ (288.7 eV) functional groups, respectively. Additionally, the specific surface area of synthesized Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC is measured to be $21.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) test and the sum of the pore volume is $0.075~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . Moreover, the corresponded $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm curve is shown in Fig. 3h and the pore distribution peak displayed in Fig. 3i is focus on $13.8\mathrm{nm}$ .
Fig. 4 shows the intrinsic atomic structure of the prepared Zn-MOF from different views, indicating that the synthesized MOF is a network structure consisting of layer sheets. Each layer consists of zinc chains connected by PTA ligands.42 Usually, each zinc atom is coordinated to a DMF molecule, forming a square cone coordination geometry. the cohesion and stability of the MOF should be attributed to the covalent bonding of the adjacent layers to the (100) plane. Moreover, the interlayer spaces are different in different directions, so that the closed-loop framework structure formed is not an equilateral quadrilateral. As can be seen from Fig. 4a, the gap between each layer is $1.06\mathrm{nm}$ , but the gap of the other layer is about $1.18~\mathrm{nm}$ . This asymmetric MOF structure not only provides more active centers, but also protects the stable phase to have enough voids for diffusion and storage of electrolyte. Its fast ion diffusion rate can further improve the electrochemical performance.
Fig. 5. Measurements of electrochemical properties through a three-electrode system. (a) CV curves of ZnO@CC, Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrodes tested at constant scan rate of 50 $\mathrm{{\mV}\ s^{-1}}$ . (b) GCD curves of ZnO@CC, Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrodes measured at constant current density of 1 mA cm-2. (c) CV and (d) GCD curves of 0.3Ni/ZnMOF/ZnO@CC electrode tested at diverse scan rates and various current densities, respectively. (e) The capacitance of the fabricated ZnO@CC, Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrodes at diverse current densities. (f) The Nyquist plots of $Z_{\mathrm{nO}\ @\mathrm{CC}}$ , Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and 0.3Ni/ZnMOF/ZnO@CC electrodes. (g) The electrochemcial stability and coulombic efficiency of the 0.3Ni/ZnMOF/ZnO@CC electrode tested at constant current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . (h) The SEM images of 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC obtained after electrochemical stability test. (i) The illustration explaining why the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode has enhanced electrochemical properties.
The electrochemical performances of the fabricated binder-free electrodes based on ZnO@CC, ZnMOF/ZnO@CC and $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Zn-MOF/ZnO}@\mathrm{CC}$ samples were all measured through the common threeelectrode electrochemical equipment. Fig. 5a exhibits the CV curves of the fabricated electrodes, which are tested at constant scan rate of $50\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ . As observed, the synthesized 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode displays a maximum closed area and highest current intensity, which represents the best supercapacitance and the fastest kinetic rate of redox. Meanwhile, the gradually increased electrochemical performance among all samples reveals the obvious synergistic effect, indicating the successful nickel ions doping shows important effects in the reaction activity and property. Additionally, Fig. 5b exhibits the GCD curves of all fabricated sample electrodes at constant current density of $1\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . As seen, the prepared 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode exhibits longer discharge time than any other electrodes, which evidenced the excellent capacitance. Moreover, the areal capacitance of 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode was calculated to be $1693~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ by using the equation (1) under the current density of 1 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is much higher than any other electrodes. Additionally, it can be observed from the CV and GCD curves that the capacitance changes along with the contents of nickel and terephthalic acid.
The electrochemical performances of 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode were further studied. As shown in Fig. 5c, it is apparent that the peak intensity and enclosed area CV areas are gradually enlarged with the increasing scan rates. Simultaneously, there is no significant change in the shape of CV curve, implying the superior electrochemical reversibility. Additionally, the galvanostatic charge-discharge curves tested at diverse current densities were used to assess the capacitance property and rate stability of the 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode. The nearly symmetrical shapes showed a high columbic efficiency of the fabricated electrode. Additionally, CV and GCD curves of other fabricated electrodes displayed in Fig. S2-5 also exhibited similar shapes and tendencies to those of 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode. As shown in Fig. 5e, the electrochemical capacitance of the 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode is calculated to be $1693\ \mathrm{mF\cm^{-2}}$ $(425\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}};$ ) under the current density of 1 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (0.25 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ), respectively, which is higher than any other fabricated electrodes and superior to or comparable with most of the recent reported electrodes based on zinc-relative materials for supercapacitors as shown in Table S1.
The electrochemical resistances of the fabricated electrodes were then measured by the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy. Each nyquist plot consists of a semicircle and a straight line in the designated frequency region. The internal resistance $(R_{s})$ is generally calculated from the intercept on the x axis. As observed, the $R_{s}$ values of the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO $@$ CC (0.89 Ω) is relative smaller than that of ZnMOF/ZnO@CC (0.91 Ω) and $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{CC}\left(1.21\Omega\right)$ , which may be due to the unique oriented structure and the doped nickel ions. In fact, the laminated scale-like and highly-oriented nanosheets are conducive to accelerate the efficient diffusion of electrolyte, thus enhancing the faradaic charge transfer rate. Moreover, in the middle frequency region, the observed semicircle domain of the Nyquist plot refers to the faradaic reactions and the diameter value refers to the interfacial charge transfer resistance $(R_{c t})$ . As seen from the curve, the semicircle of the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode is small, which can be explained by the low charge transfer resistance at the interface between electrode and electrolyte. In the designated low frequency region, the straight line of curve means the Warburg impedance $(Z_{w})$ and its steep slope represents the ions diffusion resistance in the active materials during redox reactions. From the above, the nickel ion not only plays an important key in adjusting the morphology but also provide an essential role on the electrochemical performance.
The excellent electrochemical performances of the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode may be attributed to the unique laminated scale-like and highly-oriented nanosheets structure as shown in Fig. 5i. As schematically illustrated in Fig. 5i these merits also allow the laminated scale-like and highly-oriented Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode to have superior electrochemical properties. Firstly, the carbon cloth substrate possesses porous architecture and smooth surface, which can be utilized as the attachment points for the growth of high-quality loading active materials. Secondly, the direct synthesis of Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO on fiber with strong adhesion could significantly enhance mechanical integrity during the electrochemical process without fading. Thirdly, the laminated scale-like and highly-oriented Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC nanosheets with high open channels could provide more paths for electrolyte accessing, resulting the enhanced electrochemical kinetics and the large areal capacity.
The cycling reversibility of the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode was also evaluated under constant current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and the capacitance retention can remain $80.7\%$ when compared with the initial capacity even after 2500 cycling numbers. In addition, $91.3\%$ of the coulombic efficiency can be retained, which indicates the satisfactory reversibility of the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode. Additionally, the shape of GCD curves obtained from the last several cycles are still similar to those from the first several cycles as shown in the inset of Fig. 5g, confirming the excellent electrochemical reversibility. Moreover, the SEM images of the Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode after 2500 cycles shown in Fig. 5h show that the structure is well retained, confirming the excellent durability of the prepared Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode.
