LED indicator powered by two fabricated supercapacitor units and the brightness variation within eight minutes.
To better investigate the practical application of the synthesized composites, an asymmetric solid state supercapacitor device was made on basis of the synthesized Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC. Fig. 6a displays the CV curves of active carbon and Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrodes tested through a three-electrode electrochemical system. From the above CV curves, it can be found that both the AC and Ni/ZnMOF/ZnO@CC electrodes make contributions to the total capacitance of the fabricated ASC equipment. Additionally, Fig. 6b and c show the CV and GCD curves of the fabricated ASC device tested at diverse voltages at constant scan rate of $50\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ and current density of $0.2\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ , respectively. As observed, the voltage of the fabricated device can reach $1.7\mathrm{V}$ , nevertheless, the curves still keep the initial shapes even in diverse voltage windows, which indicates the ASC devices have good capacitance performance. The CV curves of the prepared ASC device are displayed in Fig. 6d. The expanded potential window could significantly reduce the number of the ASCs to drive a device requires certain potential. The GCD curves of the device measured at diverse current densities are displayed in Fig. 6d. According to equation (4), the supercapacitance of the fabricated capacitor device was obtained. Moreover, the energy and corresponded power density were calculated according to the charge/discharge curves and shown in in Fig. 6g. In the 0 - $1.7\mathrm{V}$ working voltage range, the maximum energy density of the fabricated capacitor unit is 16.4 W h $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ at corresponded power density of 170 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ and 7.9 W h $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ at $850\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , which is superior to or comparative with some of those previously reported based on MOF electrode materials.59, 68, 69, 74, 75 In addition, the cycling stability and columbic efficiency were also tested. The capacitance retention can reach up to $76\%$ after 2500 cycling numbers and the coulombic efficiency still maintain about $86\%$ . Additionally, the schematic diagram of the ASC device fabricated using Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC and activated carbon with the assistance of gelatinous electrolyte is shown in the inset of Fig. 6i. Additionally, as shown in Fig. 6i, a $2.5\mathrm{V}$ blue LED indicator can be powered for 8 minutes by two connected capacitor units, confirming its promising practical applications.
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
To sum up, a facile strategy for the preparation of laminated scale-like and highly-oriented Ni/ZnMOF/ZnO@CC composite based on the vertically oriented $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorod arrays was proposed. The addition of nickel ions plays significant effects on the structure formation, which can implant into the framework during the recrystallization procedure. Hence, the prepared 0.3Ni/Zn-MOF/ZnO@CC electrode exhibits the capacitance of $1693~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ at the current density of $1\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . In addition, the cycling reversibility retention of $80.7\%$ after 2500 cycling numbers is obtained under given constant current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and a low internal resistance ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{s}}$ of $0.89\Omega$ is observed. Furthermore, the solid-state asymmetric supercapacitor equipment based on the as-synthesized laminated scale-like Ni/ZnMOF/ZnO@CC nanocomposite is successfully fabricated and exhibits an energy density of 16.4 W h kg-1 at corresponded power density of $170\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . For practical application, when two fabricated solid-state supercapacitor units are connected in series, a 2.5V indicator can be powered for eight minutes. Overall, this work demonstrates a promising potential of the synthesized novel Zn-MOF based nanocomposites for electrochemical energy storage applications.
